1. She has a strong preference for chicken burgers, particularly McDonald's chicken burgers. (Verbatim: "I really like chicken burgers right because the McDonald's chicken burgers just so heck and yummy")
2. She enjoys homemade chicken burgers with wedges and a salad. (Verbatim: "mine is better mine is a homemade chicken burger and wedges and a salad")
3. She has not tried an American-style burger but is eager to do so. (Verbatim: "I need to try one one day I need to try one one day")
4. She finds the size of large burgers overwhelming and uncomfortable. (Verbatim: "I can't handle the little burger like anymore my heart gets upset it's like oh gosh this is bad for me")
5. She is unfamiliar with Wendy's and believes it is not common in England. (Verbatim: "I've personally not seen a Wendy's in England I think we do have them I just don't know where")
6. She has never been to London and finds it intimidating due to its size, cost, and perceived danger. (Verbatim: "I don't think I could live in London I'll be honest it's too busy and scary and expensive")
7. She once stayed in a dangerous area of London where people were eating out of bins. (Verbatim: "I stayed in somewhere very rough and there were people eating out of the bins")
8. She is aware of London's zoning system for public transport and the associated cost differences. (Verbatim: "you get charged on the tube depending on like which zones you've traveled to")
9. She compares the London tube system to Japan's train system, noting it is less quiet and more chaotic. (Verbatim: "In Japan it was just so quiet with so many people... in London it's really loud even with less people")
10. She has a negative experience with loud drunk groups on trains in England. (Verbatim: "There's always always always at least one group of really loud drunks") [end of text]



1. She is of mixed race. (Verbatim: "oh you know you know that you know you didn't ask for your number because you're pretty it's just because you're mixed race and i was like what excuse me um that's racist to say um")
